rew two lines in front of the triangular mark indicating the mountain top, one of which was a river and the other was a road that abruptly ended. [16] \"This place is a hidden gem,\" said the friend, grinning. [17] However, when they arrived in Yamauchi Village after several bus transfers, they were surprised to find that it was even smaller than they had imagined. The village, nestled in a low-lying valley surrounded by steep mountains, was sparsely populated with scattered houses. The river flowing through the village divided it in two, with only a small patch of flat land on which rice paddies and terraced fields were laid out. The two hills on either side approached from above, their snow-capped peaks obscuring the sky. They got off the bus and asked a local for directions. The villager looked at them curiously and pointed upstream along the river. When they asked for an explanation, the villager said that although a few tourists came to the hot spring for healing, they all drove there. It was only natural, as the road to the inn was not accessible by public transportation, and Kenji Maeda and his friend had to hike for about thirty minutes with their backpacks. [18] That's all the impression Kenji Maeda has of Kotoyu Onsen from two years ago. He remembers the route well, but he can't recall the exact structure of the inn or the outdoor hot spring. Everything was old and well-maintained, but there were no other guests, so the atmosphere was excellent \u2013 that's all that remains in his foggy memory. Despite the hot spring and inn being good, they didn't leave a lasting impression. Since it's a small village, there were no opportunities to work, so they left hastily after staying for just one night. This is why the memory is vague. The run-down hot spring inn was far from being a gold mine. [19] Therefore, just a month ago, Kotoyu Onsen was almost forgotten by Kenji Maeda. He suddenly remembered Kotoyu Onsen because of the old story the innkeeper told him. [20]